  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "The full paper text is present. Some tables and figures are flattened in the supplied text.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper evaluates causal feature-disentanglement methods in Llama2-7B, providing indirect evidence about distributed features and SAE-recovered features in an LLM, using a different method and benchmark from the toy-model superposition claim.",
    "gate_note": "The paper cites polysemanticity and Toy Models of Superposition as background, but reports no new toy-network experiments or tests of feature sparsity, importance, or capacity pressure. Rows cover the reported aggregate benchmark scores and explicit textual findings relevant to distributed or SAE features. The per-attribute breakdown in Table 6 is not separately extracted.",
    "test_reason": "RAVEL is a benchmark of interpretability methods. Every result is a method's interchange-intervention Disentangle score for isolating entity attributes in Llama2-7B, including the authors' own MDAS. It cites polysemanticity and Toy Models as motivation but does not measure how many features are stored per neuron or dimension, sparsity or capacity effects, or whether SAE features are canonical units. The gap between distributed and neuron-level methods (DAS 56.5 vs DBM 52.2; MDAS 60.1 vs MDBM 53.7, Entity split) and the low SAE scores bear only loosely on superposition. They are recorded under fits_no_version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right. The authors conclude that MDAS is best on RAVEL and that methods should identify features distributed across neurons. Counterfactually supervised methods beat unsupervised ones (PCA and SAE score lowest), and some attribute pairs stay entangled across all methods. City attributes disentangle gradually across layers in Llama2-7B.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "This is an LLM interpretability benchmark using interchange interventions to evaluate attribute disentanglement methods, not the toy-network superposition method.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Outcome varies by interpretability method, entity versus context split, attribute pair, feature dimensionality, and layer.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Distributed-subspace methods (DAS 56.5/57.3, MDAS 60.1/65.6) beat neuron-level masking (DBM 52.2/49.8, MDBM 53.7/53.9) on Entity/Context. The authors call this evidence that features are distributed across neurons.",
        "proposed_claim": "Attribute information in LLM residual streams is better isolated by rotated subspaces than by selecting individual neurons.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r5",
          "r6",
          "r7",
          "r8",
          "r13",
          "r14",
          "r15",
          "r16"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "An SAE (16384 latents on Llama2-7B residual stream) with classifier-selected latents scores low on attribute isolation (48.6/46.8): high Iso but very low Cause in Table 6.",
        "proposed_claim": "SAE latents selected for an attribute often fail to carry that attribute's causal effect under interchange intervention.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r3",
          "r11"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "controls": "n",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "https://github.com/explanare/ravel",
    "peer_reviewed": "Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics",
    "problems": "The evidence is based on Llama2-7B only; intervention sites are restricted to the residual stream above the last entity token. Results are single-run test-set scores, with no uncertainty estimates reported. The authors state that different architectures or training paradigms may favor different methods.",
    "rows_left_out": "The per-attribute score breakdowns in Table 6 and additional method-specific visualizations in Figures 2–4 are not separately extracted; the record keeps aggregate benchmark results and textual findings that bear most directly on distributed or SAE-recovered features.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "No toy models, no sparsity sweep, no feature geometry."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "No test of whether capacity pressure drives polysemanticity."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "No test of non-capacity causes of polysemanticity."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "Does not measure how many neurons a feature spans, and does not test whether the model contains SAE-recoverable sparse features. It only compares method scores at isolating attributes."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "The SAE is used as one featurizer among several. Its completeness, stability and canonicity are not tested."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "Feature dimensionality is a method hyperparameter. There is no test of whether superposed features are 1-D or multi-dimensional."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [],
    "verdict_notes": "Classed as a method benchmark (T4), not a test of superposition. The neuron-vs-subspace and SAE results are only suggestive and are confounded by differences in method objectives. Single run, Llama2-7B only, residual stream at the last entity token only."
  },
